  <section id="work-first">
    <h2>Why start with the work, not the tool?</h2>
    <p>Because the tool is rarely what decides whether AI pays off. The way the work is done
       decides it. If you start from a product demonstration, you end up looking for a place to
       put the product. If you start from the work, you find the places where help is needed and
       then ask what kind of help fits.</p>
    <p>The research points the same way. In a survey of 1,000 executives in 59 countries,
       Boston Consulting Group found that 74% of companies had yet to show tangible value from
       AI, and that the companies getting value put most of their effort into people and
       processes, not into the technology (<a href={BCG}>BCG, 24 October 2024</a>).</p>

    <BarChart
      id="chart-bcg-effort"
      title="Where AI leaders put their effort, per BCG (share of resources)"
      measure="Share of resources"
      max={100}
      bars={[
        { label: 'People and processes', value: 70, note: '%' },
        { label: 'Technology and data', value: 20, note: '%' },
        { label: 'Algorithms', value: 10, note: '%' },
      ]}
      source={{ text: 'Boston Consulting Group press release, 24 October 2024: survey of 1,000 executives in 59 countries, which also found 74% of companies have yet to show tangible value from AI.', href: BCG }}
    />

    <p>Closer to home, Deloitte reported in June 2026 that 84% of organisations in its survey of
       more than 3,200 leaders in 24 countries had not yet redesigned jobs or workflows around AI
       (<a href={DELOITTE}>Deloitte Middle East, 4 June 2026</a>). The lesson for a business in Dubai,
       Abu Dhabi, Riyadh or Doha is simple: look at the work before you buy the tool.</p>
    <p>Zaaheen is an independent AI consultancy based in Dubai that assesses businesses across
       the UAE, Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Kuwait, Bahrain and Oman on site, role by role. We sell no
       tools and take no commissions. This guide is the method we use, written so you can try
       it yourself.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="map">
    <h2>How do you map how a team really works?</h2>
    <p>Sit with the people doing the job and write down what they actually do, step by step,
       with a time against each step. Not what the procedure manual says, and not what the
       manager thinks happens. The workarounds, the copying between screens and the waiting for
       an answer are where most of the time goes, and they rarely appear on paper.</p>
    <p>A useful map, which doubles as a simple AI readiness checklist, records for every step:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>What triggers it.</strong> An email arrives, a form is filed, a customer calls.</li>
      <li><strong>Who does it.</strong> The role, not the person's name.</li>
      <li><strong>How long it takes</strong>, and how many times a day or a month it happens.</li>
      <li><strong>Where the information comes from</strong> and where it goes next.</li>
      <li><strong>Hand-offs and waiting points</strong>, where the work sits with someone else.</li>
      <li><strong>Exceptions</strong>, the cases that do not follow the usual path.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Do this for one team at a time: reception, customer service, sales, finance, HR,
       operations, procurement, IT, risk and compliance. A finance team at month end and a
       customer service desk on a Sunday morning look very different, so watch on ordinary days
       and busy ones.</p>
    <p>Once the steps are timed, multiply time by volume. A two-minute task done 400 times a
       month is over thirteen hours of someone's month. A one-hour task done twice a year is not
       worth changing at all.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="candidates">
    <h2>Which steps are good candidates for AI?</h2>
    <p>Steps that read, write, sort or answer, at volume, where the data already exists. Score
       each step on five things and the candidates stand out:</p>
    <table class="learn-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Score</th><th scope="col">Points towards AI</th><th scope="col">Points away from AI</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Volume</th><td>Happens many times a day</td><td>A few times a month</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Repetition</th><td>Much the same each time</td><td>Different every time</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Rules</th><td>Needs reading and judgement on language, like an email or a document</td><td>Fixed rules a form could apply (that is automation, not AI)</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Data</th><td>The information is already written down and reachable</td><td>It lives in someone's head or on paper</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Cost of a mistake</th><td>Low, or a person checks before it goes out</td><td>High and hard to undo</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Typical steps that score well: sorting a shared inbox, drafting replies to common
       questions, pulling details out of invoices or contracts, summarising a long case for the
       next person, or answering staff questions about internal policy. Each one is high volume,
       mostly language, and based on information the business already holds.</p>

    <MemoryFlow
      id="flow-score-step"
      from="Sales inbox"
      to="AI now"
      fact="Reply to price request"
      hub="Process map"
      hubNote="timed, scored step"
      saved="Verdict given"
      caption="Each step is timed and scored before anyone picks a tool. A price request in the sales inbox, frequent and based on a known price list, scores well and becomes an AI now verdict."
    />

    <p>People often ask which department should use AI first. The honest answer is whichever
       team has the most high-scoring hours, and that is usually found by counting, not guessing.
       It may be customer service or finance; sometimes it is a team nobody thought of.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="automation">
    <h2>When is simple automation better than AI?</h2>
    <p>When the step follows fixed rules and the inputs are clean. A form, a workflow or a link
       between two systems will do it cheaper and safer than AI, and it gives the same answer
       every time. Copying a confirmed order from email into the accounts system, sending a
       reminder three days before a payment is due, or routing a leave request to the right
       manager: none of these needs AI.</p>
    <p>There is a third answer that often matters more than either: fix the process first. If a
       step has too many exceptions, unclear ownership or missing data, AI on top of it only
       makes it break faster. Sometimes the best change is agreeing who owns a task, or removing
       a step that exists only because of an old system.</p>
    <p>We explain the line between the two in more detail in
       <a href="/learn/ai-vs-automation/">our guide to AI vs automation</a>,
       and RAND's study of 65 practitioners found that the most common reason AI projects fail is
       that the problem was misunderstood in the first place (<a href={RAND}>RAND, 13 August 2024</a>).
       Our guide on <a href="/learn/why-ai-projects-fail/">why AI projects fail</a> covers that and
       three other named studies.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="people">
    <h2>What should you leave to your people?</h2>
    <p>Low-volume work, and work that needs expert judgement where a mistake is costly. A
       difficult conversation with a long-standing client, a decision on a large credit limit, a
       disciplinary matter in HR: these are rare, they depend on context, and getting one wrong
       costs far more than the time AI would save.</p>
    <p>Leaving work alone is a real result, not a failure of the exercise. Some work should stay
       with your people, and a good assessment says so. Even where AI does help, decide in
       advance where a person must stay in charge: who checks the draft before it goes to a
       customer, and who signs off a payment.</p>
    <p>This is also why every recommendation should carry numbers: the effort to set it up, a
       cost range, the hours it saves each month, and when it pays back. Our guide to
       <a href="/learn/ai-roi-before-you-buy/">working out AI's return before you buy</a> shows
       how to do that without invented benchmarks.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="yourself">
    <h2>Can you do this yourself, or do you need someone on site?</h2>
    <p>You can do a first pass yourself, and for a small team it is worth trying. Pick one team,
       sit with them for a day, time the steps and score them with the table above. You will
       learn a lot about your own business.</p>
    <p>Three things make it harder from the inside. People describe their job to their manager
       differently from how they do it. Owners are too close to their own processes to see the
       workarounds. And without an independent view, it is easy to score steps to fit a tool you
       have already seen in a demonstration.</p>
    <p>That is the gap <a href="/consulting/">our AI Readiness Assessment</a> fills. We come to
       your office, walk every role from reception to the managing director, map each process
       with timed steps and hand-offs, and score every step. Each team gets one of four verdicts:
       AI now, Automate without AI, Fix the process first, or Leave as it is. You get a written
       report and a read-out meeting with your team. If you are comparing options in the Gulf,
       <a href="/learn/ai-readiness-assessment-uae/">what an AI readiness assessment in the UAE covers</a>
       explains the visit step by step.</p>
  </section>

